    {
      "v": 7,
      "zh": "有人告诉扫罗说：「大卫到了基伊拉。」扫罗说：「他进了有门有闩的城，困闭在里头；这是　神将他交在我手里了。」",
      "en": "When Saul was told that David had come to Keilah, Saul said, “God has delivered[NOTE:1] him into my hand, for he has boxed himself into a corner by entering a city with two barred gates.”[NOTE:2]",
      "notes": [
        "The MT reading (“God has alienated him into my hand”) in v. 7 is a difficult and uncommon idiom. The use of this verb in Jer 19:4 is somewhat parallel, but not entirely so. Many scholars have therefore suspected a textual problem here, emending the word נִכַּר (nikkar, “alienated”) to סִכַּר (sikkar, “he has shut up [i.e., delivered]”). This is the idea reflected in the translations of the Syriac Peshitta and Vulgate, although it is not entirely clear whether they are reading something different from the MT or are simply paraphrasing what for them too may have been a difficult text. The LXX has “God has sold him into my hands,” apparently reading מָכַר (makar, “sold”) for MT’s נִכַּר. The present translation is a rather free interpretation.",
        "Heb “with two gates and a bar.” Since in English “bar” could be understood as a saloon, it has been translated as an attributive: “two barred gates.”"
      ],
